WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.020
The line went live last week,

2
00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.260
and the support tickets split
into two neat piles.

3
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.540
Quiet callers are fine. Loud
callers hear a harsh,

4
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.020
raspy buzz on top of their voice.

5
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.160
One says his boss sounds like
he's yelling through a kazoo.

6
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.520
Strange, isn't it? The line runs
the same code for everybody,

7
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:14.900
and louder should just be louder.

8
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.020
So look at what it
carries: the microphone's voltage,

9
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.600
measured eight thousand times a second.

10
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.300
Each measurement is a sample, and a
call is a list of numbers.

11
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:23.230
The simplest sound is a tone,

12
00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.950
like a tuning fork: a steady wave,

13
00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.350
and how many times
a second it repeats,

14
00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:27.730
in hertz, is the pitch you hear.

15
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.110
Its samples come from the cosine.

16
00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.270
For one thousand hertz, the angle steps
three hundred sixty times one thousand over

17
00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:34.550
eight thousand. That's forty-five degrees each sample,

18
00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.470
so after eight samples
the pattern starts over.

19
00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:38.730
Early in the chain sits the booster.

20
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.670
It's a system: a rule
that takes in one signal,

21
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.270
one list of numbers,
and gives back another.

22
00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:43.430
Its rule is one line of code:

23
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:44.730
multiply every sample by four.

24
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.330
But the chip after it can only
store values from minus one to plus

25
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.290
one. Hand it two, and
it writes down one.

26
00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:50.670
That flattening is called clipping.

27
00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.390
Take a quiet caller
at size point two,

28
00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:54.870
so their biggest sample is point two,

29
00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.210
and a loud one at point five.

30
00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.970
Which comes out clean? The quiet one
grows to point eight and stays inside

31
00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:00.630
the range. The loud
one would reach two,

32
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:02.230
so its tops are sliced off flat.

33
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.010
Is that just an uglier tone?

34
00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.390
Try a pencil test. In any tone,

35
00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.090
a sample's two neighbors add up to
the middle sample times a fixed number,

36
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.310
here one point four one four.

37
00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.050
The clean tone passes.
The clipped one fails.

38
00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.710
So what is it? It's two tones.

39
00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.710
A one thousand hertz tone, minus a
three thousand hertz tone a sixth its

40
00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.930
size, lands exactly on every clipped sample.

41
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.190
That second tone is the buzz.

42
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.330
Nobody said it. The booster
made it, out of nothing.

43
00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:26.470
What did the booster break?

44
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.930
A system is linear when doubling
the input doubles the output,

45
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.930
and two inputs added
give their outputs added.

46
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.470
The real booster is not linear.

47
00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.330
It only behaves like the linear times
four while its inputs stay inside the

48
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:36.350
range.

49
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.960
And here's the trap. Two
quiet voices share a speakerphone.

50
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.320
Each alone comes out at point eight,

51
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.700
fine. Linearity says together they
should give one point six.

52
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.160
The chip writes one. Each passed alone,

53
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.600
and together they buzz. Linearity
is a promise about sums.

54
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:48.920
Now add one more property.

55
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.880
A system is time-invariant when delaying
the input only delays the output.

56
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:52.900
Together they make a law:

57
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.220
a tone that goes into a linear,

58
00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.040
time-invariant system comes out
at the same pitch,

59
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.440
only bigger or smaller, and shifted.

60
00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:58.500
No new tones, ever.

61
00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.090
So the fix is to keep
every input inside the range.

62
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:03.730
The loudest input the booster ever sees,

63
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.330
speakerphones included, is point five.

64
00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:06.570
Times two lands right on the edge,

65
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:09.310
with no margin. Instead, keep the
loudest peak at point eight,

66
00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.330
a fifth below the edge.
That gap is the headroom.

67
00:02:11.477 --> 00:02:13.650
Point eight divided by point
five is one point six.

68
00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.910
Now the buzz meter, which measures the
tones that weren't in the input,

69
00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.430
reads zero percent on every caller.

70
00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.850
Loud callers sound like loud people again.

71
00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.590
Then a new ticket arrives:
I can hear myself.

72
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.650
Every word comes back, faint, a
quarter of a second later.

73
00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:25.670
That's the next bug.
